    #   (x * 1.25) + (y * 0.75) - 0.125, then relu.
    # The baseline materialised this as mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone (6 GPU
    # kernels + 6 python dispatches, one full extra device copy). Here the whole
    # chain is accumulated in ONE freshly allocated buffer using in-place ops,
    # so it costs 4 kernels and 4 dispatches, and no redundant copy:
    #   1) out   = x * 1.25            (out-of-place, allocates)
    #   2) out  += 0.75 * y            (add_ with alpha, no temporary)
    #   3) out  -= 0.125
    #   4) out   = max(out, 0)         (in-place relu, returns the same buffer)
    # Folding 0.75 into add_'s alpha removes the separate y*0.75 multiply; the
    # only numeric effect is that alpha*b may be contracted with the add (<=0.5
    # ulp on values <= 10, i.e. ~1e-6), far inside atol/rtol = 1e-5.
    # Inputs are never written to: step 1 always produces a new tensor.
    out = inputs['x'] * 1.25
    out.add_(inputs['y'], alpha=0.75).sub_(0.125).clamp_min_(0)
    return out
